1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,090
Pancakes, just as promised.

2
00:00:02,240 --> 00:00:06,720
I am ready to help. Sensible quantities, no
size records.

3
00:00:06,870 --> 00:00:09,590
Then breakfast has an excellent start.

4
00:00:09,740 --> 00:00:13,780
Start with the recipe, then. The bowl can
wait.

5
00:00:15,250 --> 00:00:17,970
Dan, could you get the flour?

6
00:00:18,120 --> 00:00:21,280
Of course. I know just the thing.

7
00:00:21,430 --> 00:00:24,150
Why are you looking over there?

8
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:27,900
I'm picturing something with a nice long stem.

9
00:00:30,250 --> 00:00:33,648
How large a bouquet do pancakes need?

10
00:00:33,798 --> 00:00:34,831
A bouquet?

11
00:00:34,981 --> 00:00:37,906
Would breakfast need a flowerpot too?

12
00:00:38,056 --> 00:00:42,400
Dad, what would a bouquet taste like with
syrup?

13
00:00:45,250 --> 00:00:49,481
Flour for baking, Dan. Not a flower from the
garden.

14
00:00:49,631 --> 00:00:53,031
Ah. The pancakes are not requesting a
centerpiece.

15
00:00:53,181 --> 00:00:54,503
Good catch, Mom.

16
00:00:54,653 --> 00:00:59,300
I did wonder how we'd get petals out of our
teeth.

17
00:01:00,250 --> 00:01:02,970
So the garden keeps its flowers?

18
00:01:03,120 --> 00:01:06,720
Every one. Our breakfast needs a baking
ingredient.

19
00:01:06,870 --> 00:01:10,030
The vase has been excused from cooking.

20
00:01:10,180 --> 00:01:14,220
It looks awfully pleased to be off the menu.

21
00:01:15,250 --> 00:01:19,481
The recipe says two cups. That is a useful
clue.

22
00:01:19,631 --> 00:01:22,616
And it belongs in the mixing bowl.

23
00:01:22,766 --> 00:01:25,334
I should read the whole invitation.

24
00:01:25,484 --> 00:01:29,300
Two cups of the stuff in the pantry, please.

25
00:01:30,250 --> 00:01:33,850
I heard one word and guessed the rest.

26
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:36,720
You can ask what we mean.

27
00:01:36,870 --> 00:01:39,590
We have time to check together.

28
00:01:39,740 --> 00:01:43,780
Okay. Where would you look for the baking
kind?

29
00:01:45,250 --> 00:01:48,650
Let us find what the recipe actually needs.

30
00:01:48,800 --> 00:01:50,953
I can read the list.

31
00:01:51,103 --> 00:01:54,919
I will measure. Max can help find the tools.

32
00:01:55,069 --> 00:01:59,300
Can I find something that looks like a pancake
tool?

33
00:02:00,250 --> 00:02:02,530
This flour is for pancakes.

34
00:02:02,680 --> 00:02:04,960
Yes. Same sound, different meaning.

35
00:02:05,110 --> 00:02:07,830
The recipe and the label agree.

36
00:02:07,980 --> 00:02:12,460
Well, I've never seen a flower come in a bag.

37
00:02:15,250 --> 00:02:16,816
Is the vase disappointed?

38
00:02:16,966 --> 00:02:20,405
It prefers a quiet shelf to a hot stove.

39
00:02:20,555 --> 00:02:22,122
A very sensible vase.

40
00:02:22,272 --> 00:02:25,711
It won't have to wash up after breakfast,
either.

41
00:02:25,861 --> 00:02:29,300
And I don't have to find it a fork.

42
00:02:30,250 --> 00:02:33,410
A big bowl and a smaller bowl.

43
00:02:33,560 --> 00:02:36,280
Dry ingredients here. Wet ingredients there.

44
00:02:36,430 --> 00:02:39,150
They have different jobs, like us.

45
00:02:39,300 --> 00:02:43,340
The yellow one's easier for me to see into.

46
00:02:45,250 --> 00:02:48,329
That spoon has a lot of holes.

47
00:02:48,479 --> 00:02:52,414
It is a whisk. For mixing, not carrying soup.

48
00:02:52,564 --> 00:02:55,214
Good. We found our first helper.

49
00:02:55,364 --> 00:02:59,300
I thought our soup would just fall right
through.

50
00:03:00,250 --> 00:03:03,850
A tablespoon and a half teaspoon. Different
measures.

51
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,280
So not just any spoon?

52
00:03:06,430 --> 00:03:10,030
Exactly. These help us use the right amount.

53
00:03:10,180 --> 00:03:14,220
Read the handle. That's where the size is
marked.

54
00:03:15,250 --> 00:03:18,454
The marks are easier to read down here.

55
00:03:18,604 --> 00:03:22,200
Keep the measuring cup flat, then check the
line.

56
00:03:22,350 --> 00:03:25,554
My eyes can do some of the work.

57
00:03:25,704 --> 00:03:29,300
And the milk won't slope away from the mark.

58
00:03:30,250 --> 00:03:33,410
That is the spatula for turning pancakes.

59
00:03:33,560 --> 00:03:34,960
Not the whisk?

60
00:03:35,110 --> 00:03:38,710
Correct. The pancakes would prefer a flat
ride.

61
00:03:38,860 --> 00:03:42,900
Right. A whisk would leave funny stripes on
breakfast.

62
00:03:45,250 --> 00:03:48,146
The pan and stove are adult jobs.

63
00:03:48,296 --> 00:03:51,595
The children have their space at the island.

64
00:03:51,745 --> 00:03:55,044
And we have a clear path between them.

65
00:03:55,194 --> 00:03:59,300
Let's keep that space open once the pan gets
warm.

66
00:04:00,250 --> 00:04:02,043
Sugar, baking powder, salt.

67
00:04:02,193 --> 00:04:04,414
No decorations on that list.

68
00:04:04,564 --> 00:04:09,357
Only ingredients. Let us check each label
before we open it.

69
00:04:09,507 --> 00:04:14,300
These names look similar when you grab them in
a hurry.

70
00:04:15,250 --> 00:04:18,410
Milk, eggs and butter. Everything from home.

71
00:04:18,560 --> 00:04:22,160
So breakfast does not need another shopping
trip.

72
00:04:22,310 --> 00:04:24,590
Just a little careful preparation.

73
00:04:24,740 --> 00:04:28,780
Good. I'm hungry enough to stay in this
kitchen.

74
00:04:30,250 --> 00:04:33,410
Twelve pancakes. Two each for six people.

75
00:04:33,560 --> 00:04:36,280
A breakfast with a clear plan.

76
00:04:36,430 --> 00:04:39,150
We can make every pancake count.

77
00:04:39,300 --> 00:04:43,340
Two each sounds much better than one enormous
one.

78
00:04:45,250 --> 00:04:48,850
Before I reach for anything, what comes first?

79
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:51,280
Clean hands. Then we measure.

80
00:04:51,430 --> 00:04:54,590
That is a much better opening question.

81
00:04:54,740 --> 00:04:58,780
Then my very first ingredient is a little
patience.

82
00:05:00,250 --> 00:05:03,410
Even the helpers who only carry napkins?

83
00:05:03,560 --> 00:05:05,840
Everyone starts with clean hands.

84
00:05:05,990 --> 00:05:09,590
My hands have passed their first breakfast
test.

85
00:05:09,740 --> 00:05:13,780
Mine smell like soap. They're ready for napkin
duty.

86
00:05:15,250 --> 00:05:18,410
Blue for dry ingredients. Yellow for wet.

87
00:05:18,560 --> 00:05:20,400
One batch, two jobs.

88
00:05:20,550 --> 00:05:23,710
And the right measure beside each bowl.

89
00:05:23,860 --> 00:05:27,900
Leo, can you read the first amount for us?

90
00:05:30,250 --> 00:05:33,410
A level cup, not a little mountain.

91
00:05:33,560 --> 00:05:35,400
My mountains are retiring.

92
00:05:35,550 --> 00:05:38,710
Good. This recipe needs an even top.

93
00:05:38,860 --> 00:05:42,900
Flat across, then. No snowy peaks on this
breakfast.

94
00:05:45,250 --> 00:05:47,530
One cup, then another. Two.

95
00:05:47,680 --> 00:05:49,520
Exactly what we need.

96
00:05:49,670 --> 00:05:53,270
Now the bag can rest. No third mountain.

97
00:05:53,420 --> 00:05:57,460
I liked counting the mountains better than
carrying them.

98
00:06:00,250 --> 00:06:02,090
Two tablespoons of sugar.

99
00:06:02,240 --> 00:06:05,400
Not two cups. I read that carefully.

100
00:06:05,550 --> 00:06:08,270
A very helpful kind of careful.

101
00:06:08,420 --> 00:06:12,460
Two cups of sugar would make an interesting
morning.

102
00:06:15,250 --> 00:06:17,530
Baking powder. The names matter.

103
00:06:17,680 --> 00:06:19,960
And the amount matters too.

104
00:06:20,110 --> 00:06:23,710
One tablespoon. Let us measure before we pour.

105
00:06:23,860 --> 00:06:28,340
I'll keep my finger on that line while you do.

106
00:06:30,250 --> 00:06:32,530
One tablespoon is three teaspoons.

107
00:06:32,680 --> 00:06:35,400
The same amount in different measures.

108
00:06:35,550 --> 00:06:38,710
Exactly. We add it once, not twice.

109
00:06:38,860 --> 00:06:42,900
Good. I'd rather count the spoons than start
over.

110
00:06:45,250 --> 00:06:48,850
Half a teaspoon. This is the tiny helper.

111
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:51,720
Small amounts can do important jobs.

112
00:06:51,870 --> 00:06:54,590
All four dry ingredients are here.

113
00:06:54,740 --> 00:06:59,220
Was that tiny scoop really all the salt it
needed?

114
00:07:00,250 --> 00:07:03,410
Three for batter. One for the pan.

115
00:07:03,560 --> 00:07:04,960
Four tablespoons altogether.

116
00:07:05,110 --> 00:07:09,590
We divide it now so it cannot get lost later.

117
00:07:09,740 --> 00:07:13,780
I'll remember. Three go in, one stays back
here.

118
00:07:15,250 --> 00:07:18,410
A small pan for a small job.

119
00:07:18,560 --> 00:07:21,720
Low heat, then we let it cool.

120
00:07:21,870 --> 00:07:24,590
The butter gets a quiet morning.

121
00:07:24,740 --> 00:07:28,780
And back from the stove till the butter cools.

122
00:07:30,250 --> 00:07:34,066
We can mix the dry ingredients while it cools.

123
00:07:34,216 --> 00:07:38,031
So waiting still gives us something useful to
do.

124
00:07:38,181 --> 00:07:39,919
Exactly. Carefully, not quickly.

125
00:07:40,069 --> 00:07:44,300
The blue bowl can have our attention in the
meantime.

126
00:07:45,250 --> 00:07:48,650
Two eggs. No shell seats in the bowl.

127
00:07:48,800 --> 00:07:50,953
Correct. The shells go here.

128
00:07:51,103 --> 00:07:54,919
And the dry bowl stays over on its mat.

129
00:07:55,069 --> 00:07:59,300
Good. Nobody wants a crunchy bit of shell at
breakfast.

130
00:08:00,250 --> 00:08:03,410
The shells are away. What is next?

131
00:08:03,560 --> 00:08:06,280
Clean hands again, then the milk.

132
00:08:06,430 --> 00:08:10,030
A useful step worth keeping in the plan.

133
00:08:10,180 --> 00:08:14,220
Yes. I'll meet you back here with clean hands.

134
00:08:15,250 --> 00:08:18,846
One and a half cups, right to the line.

135
00:08:18,996 --> 00:08:21,417
Check it here at eye level.

136
00:08:21,567 --> 00:08:25,163
Perfect. I will pour it into the wet bowl.

137
00:08:25,313 --> 00:08:29,300
From up high I thought it needed a little
more.

138
00:08:30,250 --> 00:08:34,237
The rest of the milk goes back in the fridge.

139
00:08:34,387 --> 00:08:37,200
Yes. We only measured what we needed.

140
00:08:37,350 --> 00:08:40,163
And the jug has finished its job.

141
00:08:40,313 --> 00:08:44,300
Good thing our fridge is closer than the fruit
shop.

142
00:08:45,250 --> 00:08:47,970
Eggs, milk, and the cooled butter.

143
00:08:48,120 --> 00:08:52,160
Three tablespoons. The pan still has its own
one.

144
00:08:52,310 --> 00:08:54,590
Our wet ingredients are ready.

145
00:08:54,740 --> 00:08:58,780
I like knowing exactly where the fourth
spoonful is.

146
00:09:00,250 --> 00:09:02,900
Wet ingredients into the dry bowl.

147
00:09:03,050 --> 00:09:05,700
One bowl becomes our batter bowl.

148
00:09:05,850 --> 00:09:09,357
And we keep it steady while we pour.

149
00:09:09,507 --> 00:09:14,300
A steady bowl is all the help I need right
now.

150
00:09:15,250 --> 00:09:17,819
The yellow bowl is empty now.

151
00:09:17,969 --> 00:09:20,953
Its work moved into the blue bowl.

152
00:09:21,103 --> 00:09:24,503
One batch, all together. Now we fold gently.

153
00:09:24,653 --> 00:09:29,300
I can see the yellow bowl right down to the
bottom.

154
00:09:30,250 --> 00:09:33,146
A gentle fold. Not a breakfast race.

155
00:09:33,296 --> 00:09:35,385
There are still little lumps.

156
00:09:35,535 --> 00:09:40,044
That is fine. We do not need to chase every
one.

157
00:09:40,194 --> 00:09:44,300
You're sure? That last little lump is looking
at me.

158
00:09:45,250 --> 00:09:47,090
That is enough mixing.

159
00:09:47,240 --> 00:09:49,080
I helped by stopping?

160
00:09:49,230 --> 00:09:53,270
Yes. Knowing when to stop is part of helping.

161
00:09:53,420 --> 00:09:57,460
See how the batter holds together on its own?

162
00:10:00,250 --> 00:10:02,530
Does breakfast need a nap?

163
00:10:02,680 --> 00:10:05,400
Five quiet minutes for the batter.

164
00:10:05,550 --> 00:10:09,590
Then I will help it by leaving it alone.

165
00:10:09,740 --> 00:10:14,220
I can be quiet. Do I have to nap too?

166
00:10:15,250 --> 00:10:17,607
One plate at every place.

167
00:10:17,757 --> 00:10:19,658
Six places, six plates.

168
00:10:19,808 --> 00:10:23,074
No plate needed for the flower vase.

169
00:10:23,224 --> 00:10:27,400
It can watch from the shelf while we eat.

170
00:10:30,250 --> 00:10:33,650
I found a job with no hot pan.

171
00:10:33,800 --> 00:10:36,369
And I found all six forks.

172
00:10:36,519 --> 00:10:39,503
You are making breakfast easier for everyone.

173
00:10:39,653 --> 00:10:44,300
Napkins don't need flipping, do they? That's
my kind of job.

174
00:10:45,250 --> 00:10:49,066
I am resisting a very small urge to stir.

175
00:10:49,216 --> 00:10:51,369
That looks like excellent work.

176
00:10:51,519 --> 00:10:54,919
It is. Some jobs are quieter than others.

177
00:10:55,069 --> 00:10:59,300
My hands didn't know they could be useful like
this.

178
00:11:00,250 --> 00:11:03,410
Five minutes later, our batter is ready.

179
00:11:03,560 --> 00:11:05,840
And so is the table.

180
00:11:05,990 --> 00:11:10,030
Waiting helped breakfast move forward. I like
this system.

181
00:11:10,180 --> 00:11:14,220
Just think how many extra stirs we saved you.

182
00:11:15,250 --> 00:11:17,970
Handle inward. Clear space beside us.

183
00:11:18,120 --> 00:11:20,840
And only adults at the stove.

184
00:11:20,990 --> 00:11:24,590
I can follow a very sensible cooking plan.

185
00:11:24,740 --> 00:11:29,220
Good. Stay beside me where you can see the
edges.

186
00:11:30,250 --> 00:11:33,850
This one tablespoon is for all the batches.

187
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:36,280
A little at a time.

188
00:11:36,430 --> 00:11:40,030
Exactly. A light coat, not a butter pond.

189
00:11:40,180 --> 00:11:43,780
Our pancakes can swim some other morning,
then.

190
00:11:45,250 --> 00:11:48,410
Two pancakes at a time, six batches.

191
00:11:48,560 --> 00:11:51,720
Small, even portions from this one bowl.

192
00:11:51,870 --> 00:11:54,150
And no surprise thirteenth pancake.

193
00:11:54,300 --> 00:11:57,900
That tray has exactly enough room for
breakfast.

194
00:12:00,250 --> 00:12:03,757
One round pancake. One with an adventurous
edge.

195
00:12:03,907 --> 00:12:06,986
Both can still make a good breakfast.

196
00:12:07,136 --> 00:12:10,214
Then the adventurous one can be mine.

197
00:12:10,364 --> 00:12:14,300
I'll remember. That edge will be easy to
recognize.

198
00:12:15,250 --> 00:12:16,100
Now?

199
00:12:16,250 --> 00:12:20,290
Watch the edges. Let the pan do its work.

200
00:12:20,440 --> 00:12:24,480
Another quiet job. I am getting better at
those.

201
00:12:24,630 --> 00:12:28,670
Look there, Dan. The edge is starting to hold.

202
00:12:30,250 --> 00:12:33,757
Slide underneath, then turn close to the pan.

203
00:12:33,907 --> 00:12:35,271
No flying breakfast.

204
00:12:35,421 --> 00:12:39,357
Exactly. The table is our destination, not the
ceiling.

205
00:12:39,507 --> 00:12:44,300
The ceiling already has plenty to do holding
up the lights.

206
00:12:45,250 --> 00:12:49,066
It is cooked through, even with the unusual
edge.

207
00:12:49,216 --> 00:12:51,784
Yes. Crooked does not mean wasted.

208
00:12:51,934 --> 00:12:55,334
I am glad we checked instead of guessing.

209
00:12:55,484 --> 00:12:59,300
And you know which one is yours at breakfast.

210
00:13:00,250 --> 00:13:03,410
I thought helping meant doing something
impressive.

211
00:13:03,560 --> 00:13:05,840
Doing it carefully is useful.

212
00:13:05,990 --> 00:13:08,710
Then I will try useful again.

213
00:13:08,860 --> 00:13:13,340
Good. Watch how that first pair lies on the
tray.

214
00:13:15,250 --> 00:13:17,970
Two more. Smaller movements this time.

215
00:13:18,120 --> 00:13:20,400
They have room to cook.

216
00:13:20,550 --> 00:13:24,150
The pan and I are learning to cooperate.

217
00:13:24,300 --> 00:13:28,340
I can tell. Neither pancake is touching its
neighbor.

218
00:13:30,250 --> 00:13:32,671
Slide, flip, and keep it low.

219
00:13:32,821 --> 00:13:36,025
Good. Now the second sides need their time.

220
00:13:36,175 --> 00:13:40,163
I will not rush the other half of the job.

221
00:13:40,313 --> 00:13:44,300
Good. They'll be ready together if we leave
them be.

222
00:13:45,250 --> 00:13:46,650
Four pancakes ready.

223
00:13:46,800 --> 00:13:49,520
And every one is cooked through.

224
00:13:49,670 --> 00:13:53,710
Our breakfast is growing at a very sensible
size.

225
00:13:53,860 --> 00:13:57,900
And Dan can still spot the one he's claimed.

226
00:14:00,250 --> 00:14:02,970
Same small portions. Same patient turn.

227
00:14:03,120 --> 00:14:06,720
That is how a plan becomes a habit.

228
00:14:06,870 --> 00:14:09,590
A habit that smells rather good.

229
00:14:09,740 --> 00:14:13,780
The next pair gets the same amount of space.

230
00:14:15,250 --> 00:14:17,090
Six. Are we finished?

231
00:14:17,240 --> 00:14:19,520
Halfway. Six more to cook.

232
00:14:19,670 --> 00:14:23,710
We will share breakfast when everyone has
their two.

233
00:14:23,860 --> 00:14:28,340
So the people waiting get the same breakfast I
get.

234
00:14:30,250 --> 00:14:32,530
The bowl is getting lower.

235
00:14:32,680 --> 00:14:34,520
Exactly as it should.

236
00:14:34,670 --> 00:14:38,710
No mystery refill. Just breakfast moving to
the tray.

237
00:14:38,860 --> 00:14:42,900
And the crooked one is still easy to find.

238
00:14:45,250 --> 00:14:47,819
Eight ready. Four still to come.

239
00:14:47,969 --> 00:14:50,953
The last pairs deserve the same care.

240
00:14:51,103 --> 00:14:54,919
And the first crooked one still has its place.

241
00:14:55,069 --> 00:14:59,300
Dan may be the only one rooting for that edge.

242
00:15:00,250 --> 00:15:04,481
I do not feel the need to show off now.

243
00:15:04,631 --> 00:15:07,200
You look comfortable with the work.

244
00:15:07,350 --> 00:15:09,919
That is a much nicer feeling.

245
00:15:10,069 --> 00:15:14,300
Keep that easy grip. The pancakes seem to like
it.

246
00:15:15,250 --> 00:15:19,066
The table is ready whenever the last two are.

247
00:15:19,216 --> 00:15:21,784
One pair left, not another batch.

248
00:15:21,934 --> 00:15:24,503
Then we can all begin together.

249
00:15:24,653 --> 00:15:29,300
I can hear the pan from my place at the table.

250
00:15:30,250 --> 00:15:33,650
The last two portions, from our one bowl.

251
00:15:33,800 --> 00:15:36,784
Small and even. Use what we made.

252
00:15:36,934 --> 00:15:40,334
No refill needed. The plan reaches the end.

253
00:15:40,484 --> 00:15:44,300
Tilt it a little. There's your second little
scoop.

254
00:15:45,250 --> 00:15:47,970
Twelve small pancakes, ready to share.

255
00:15:48,120 --> 00:15:50,400
Including our first adventurous one.

256
00:15:50,550 --> 00:15:54,150
Every pancake has reached the cooked tray
safely.

257
00:15:54,300 --> 00:15:58,780
I know exactly which one I'm looking for on
there.

258
00:16:00,250 --> 00:16:02,970
Breakfast is cooked. One more job.

259
00:16:03,120 --> 00:16:05,840
Stove off. Hot pan stays here.

260
00:16:05,990 --> 00:16:09,150
A good ending for the cooking part.

261
00:16:09,300 --> 00:16:13,780
Yes. We'll leave the handle turned in while it
cools.

262
00:16:15,250 --> 00:16:18,850
Raw preparation tools here. Clean serving
tools there.

263
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:20,840
Two jobs kept separate.

264
00:16:20,990 --> 00:16:25,030
And no batter left waiting for a secret
pancake.

265
00:16:25,180 --> 00:16:28,780
Good. I'll take the clean ones for serving.

266
00:16:30,250 --> 00:16:32,819
Two halves still make one pancake.

267
00:16:32,969 --> 00:16:35,538
That one is mine, as promised.

268
00:16:35,688 --> 00:16:39,919
With one whole pancake. The same two each for
everyone.

269
00:16:40,069 --> 00:16:44,300
I wouldn't trade the crooked edge for a round
one.

270
00:16:45,250 --> 00:16:50,081
The food goes to the table. The hot pan stays
behind.

271
00:16:50,231 --> 00:16:51,066
Exactly.

272
00:16:51,216 --> 00:16:55,183
Six places are waiting for what we made
together.

273
00:16:55,333 --> 00:16:59,300
Let's carry the cooked pancakes while they're
still warm.

274
00:17:00,250 --> 00:17:03,410
My unusual pancake made it to breakfast.

275
00:17:03,560 --> 00:17:05,840
Cooked, checked, and not wasted.

276
00:17:05,990 --> 00:17:08,710
It has a very satisfying story.

277
00:17:08,860 --> 00:17:12,900
And a little edge of its own to recognize.

278
00:17:15,250 --> 00:17:16,210
Two here.

279
00:17:16,360 --> 00:17:20,400
And two here. We helped those ingredients
become breakfast.

280
00:17:20,550 --> 00:17:23,710
You did. Now we finish sharing it.

281
00:17:23,860 --> 00:17:27,900
I can see the crooked one from my place.

282
00:17:30,250 --> 00:17:33,850
Two for me. Two for each of you.

283
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:35,840
Everyone has their share.

284
00:17:35,990 --> 00:17:39,150
And the tray has finished its job.

285
00:17:39,300 --> 00:17:43,340
Even the adults got two. We counted them
right.

286
00:17:45,250 --> 00:17:48,410
Six people. Twelve pancakes. Breakfast is
here.

287
00:17:48,560 --> 00:17:51,280
And no vase needed a plate.

288
00:17:51,430 --> 00:17:54,590
That is a relief for the vase.

289
00:17:54,740 --> 00:17:58,780
Can we find out what they taste like now?

290
00:18:00,250 --> 00:18:02,471
How is the adventurous one?

291
00:18:02,621 --> 00:18:06,986
Very good. It tastes like a pancake, not a
mistake.

292
00:18:07,136 --> 00:18:09,786
That was worth a little patience.

293
00:18:09,936 --> 00:18:14,300
I'm glad we kept it. I'd have missed this
bite.

294
00:18:15,250 --> 00:18:17,090
I helped make these.

295
00:18:17,240 --> 00:18:19,080
Your careful measuring helped.

296
00:18:19,230 --> 00:18:23,710
And all those small jobs made something we
could share.

297
00:18:23,860 --> 00:18:28,340
Next time I'll know which spoon to pick up
first.

298
00:18:30,250 --> 00:18:32,900
The vase did not help much.

299
00:18:33,050 --> 00:18:36,128
It helped me ask a better question.

300
00:18:36,278 --> 00:18:40,214
And it had a very peaceful morning after that.

301
00:18:40,364 --> 00:18:44,300
Maybe it could teach the batter how to nap.

302
00:18:45,250 --> 00:18:48,650
I thought asking would make me less helpful.

303
00:18:48,800 --> 00:18:51,369
It helped us understand each other.

304
00:18:51,519 --> 00:18:54,503
And it gave everyone room to help.

305
00:18:54,653 --> 00:18:59,300
That's a job I can start before I touch a
spoon.

306
00:19:00,250 --> 00:19:02,970
Dad's breakfast came in extra pieces.

307
00:19:03,120 --> 00:19:06,720
The same two pancakes, with a different shape.

308
00:19:06,870 --> 00:19:09,590
A very individual breakfast. I approve.

309
00:19:09,740 --> 00:19:13,780
I think your pancake looks happier that way,
Dad.

310
00:19:15,250 --> 00:19:17,403
My school shoes feel tight.

311
00:19:17,553 --> 00:19:20,538
We can check the fit after breakfast.

312
00:19:20,688 --> 00:19:24,919
I will start by reading the labels. That seems
useful.

313
00:19:25,069 --> 00:19:29,300
Can I pick a pair with room for my toes?

314
00:19:30,250 --> 00:19:34,897
We found the tools, made breakfast, and even
cleared the plates.

315
00:19:35,047 --> 00:19:36,784
A very successful morning.

316
00:19:36,934 --> 00:19:40,334
And we all had a part in it.

317
00:19:40,484 --> 00:19:44,300
Especially the people who knew where the flour
was.

318
00:19:45,250 --> 00:19:47,970
No bouquet. A very good breakfast.

319
00:19:48,120 --> 00:19:49,960
Exactly what we needed.

320
00:19:50,110 --> 00:19:54,150
And the pancakes never had to learn to grow.

321
00:19:54,300 --> 00:19:57,900
Good thing. We'd still be waiting for
breakfast!
